00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:15.520
 It's like a really famous part of the race.

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:17.480
 So you get three lots of people who use it.

00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:19.600
 Basically, the youngest people who are there

00:22:19.600 --> 00:22:22.280
 for the first ever year, and they're like, oh, wow.

00:22:22.280 --> 00:22:24.720
 Like, I really need to try the showers because they're

00:22:24.720 --> 00:22:25.920
 so famous.

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:27.640
 And then you get the really old ones

00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:30.880
 who just want to embrace the history of the race.

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:33.520
 And then you get a third group who use these showers.

00:22:33.520 --> 00:22:34.920
 Can you guess who they are?

00:22:34.920 --> 00:22:39.080
 People who've been injured and need to wash mud.

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:41.400
 Well, they can still use normal showers.

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:42.480
 OK.

00:22:42.480 --> 00:22:43.720
 Oh, OK.

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:45.080
 These are bicycles.

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:46.120
 The bicycles themselves?

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:46.920
 Yeah.

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:47.680
 On their own?

00:22:47.680 --> 00:22:48.920
 Yeah.

00:22:48.920 --> 00:22:50.280
 No.

00:22:50.280 --> 00:22:52.560
 Some of the race crew stuff.

00:22:52.560 --> 00:22:53.080
 It's not.

00:22:53.080 --> 00:22:57.400
 It's the ones who are sponsored by shampoo companies.

00:22:57.400 --> 00:23:00.200
 And so at the end of the race, those ones always come in

00:23:00.200 --> 00:23:02.480
 and do a little bit of a rub down with the shampoo

00:23:02.480 --> 00:23:03.640
 and then go straight after them.

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:05.800
 That's wild showers, assholes.

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:08.840
 You've just been through absolute hell for 116 miles,

00:23:08.840 --> 00:23:10.320
 and then you have to film a shampoo advert.

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:11.480
 That's like a bad dream.

00:23:11.480 --> 00:23:13.840
 How many guesses do you think we would have had to have gone to

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:15.680
 before we'd got to that?

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:19.800
 Here's a very cool bit of the road crew who work on it.

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:21.600
 So there's a team of people who are employed to maintain

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:23.720
 the cobbles, not to make them nice and smooth.

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:26.360
 It's not possible, but just to make sure they're there

00:23:26.360 --> 00:23:27.760
 and kind of serviceable.

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:31.280
 But there is a team of extra employees

00:23:31.280 --> 00:23:32.120
 who are hired.

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:36.280
 Can anybody guess what their job is?

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:39.320
 Well, in the Tour de France, they have people who are employed

00:23:39.320 --> 00:23:41.720
 because people draw penises on their grounds.

00:23:41.720 --> 00:23:45.440
 And they employ people to turn them into beautiful butterflies.

00:23:45.440 --> 00:23:46.040
 Is it that?

00:23:46.040 --> 00:23:47.480
 It's not that.

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:50.360
 These people have to-- these crew have to eat something

00:23:50.360 --> 00:23:51.560
 as part of their job.

00:23:51.560 --> 00:23:53.520
 And I corrected people to crew there.

00:23:53.520 --> 00:23:55.280
 Hmm.

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:56.080
 Good riddle.

00:23:56.080 --> 00:23:57.240
 OK, so they like the animals.

00:23:57.240 --> 00:23:58.840
 They might be animal-- good riddle, Andy.

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:00.840
 Oh, no, they have to--

00:24:00.840 --> 00:24:02.960
 I know, because they're near farms.

00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:06.000
 So there's often like horse poo and stuff on there.

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:09.040
 So they employ a load of dung beetles

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:13.480
 to push away the balls of dung just before the bikes get us.

00:24:13.480 --> 00:24:15.280
 He's got it.

00:24:15.280 --> 00:24:17.320
 It's a team of goats eating grass.

00:24:17.320 --> 00:24:19.040
 Eating the veg in between the cobbles.

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:19.960
 OK, right.

00:24:19.960 --> 00:24:20.800
 Yeah, yeah.

00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:24.920
 And the human-- the human are called the Fosad du Pavé.

00:24:24.920 --> 00:24:27.440
 Convicts of the cobbles.

00:24:27.440 --> 00:24:28.440
 How cool is that?

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:29.720
 Oh, that's like a society.

00:24:29.720 --> 00:24:31.480
 There's a human team of maintenance people, too.

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:32.520
 Yeah, they might eat in the--

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:34.560
 They're not eating the stuff.

00:24:34.560 --> 00:24:36.480
 Well, I don't think they're bound from it.

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:39.640
 But the method ones are.

00:24:39.640 --> 00:24:43.280
 So yeah, that's like a crew that you join.

00:24:43.280 --> 00:24:44.440
 It's a society.

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:46.880
 And that's not even when the race is happening.

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:47.600
 That's a year around.

00:24:47.600 --> 00:24:49.920
 They just make sure they love the cobble.

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:51.160
 So they're probably looking out for the cobble

00:24:51.160 --> 00:24:53.800
 Steelers that we're talking about.

00:24:53.800 --> 00:24:57.880
 This is a very old race, 1896.

00:24:57.880 --> 00:25:00.120
 The bicycle, the bicycle proper,

00:25:00.120 --> 00:25:02.560
 was only invented in the 1860s.

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:05.240
 So we're talking like 30, 40 years after the invention

00:25:05.240 --> 00:25:07.600
 of the actual modern bicycle that they started doing

00:25:07.600 --> 00:25:08.840
 this insane race.

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:12.080
 And even the person who did the first mapping of the route

00:25:12.080 --> 00:25:14.080
 didn't want the race to happen.

00:25:14.080 --> 00:25:15.720
 So basically, there were two creators.

00:25:15.720 --> 00:25:17.280
 There was Perez and there was Vien.

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:20.640
 And they were like, we're going to end it in Belgium or France.

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:23.440
 We don't know which way, but we can't be there at both ends.

00:25:23.440 --> 00:25:25.080
 We need someone to start or end it.

00:25:25.080 --> 00:25:27.000
 They decided Paris would start it.

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:30.160
 And they needed to get the local newspaper on their team.

00:25:30.160 --> 00:25:33.080
 And so the Paris newspaper sent a man called Paul Rousseau

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:35.960
 to actually ride what might be a route.

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:37.600
 And he did it, and he got to the end.

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:39.400
 And he was going to write immediately and say,

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:40.960
 no human should do this.

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:42.400
 This is mad.

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:43.600
 This is insane.

00:25:43.600 --> 00:25:45.120
 But then these two guys, Perez and Vien,

00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:46.520
 took him out to dinner, got him drunk.

00:25:46.520 --> 00:25:49.240
 And then he was like, yeah, I'll be fine, I'll be gone.

00:25:49.240 --> 00:25:50.480
 And that's why we have it.

00:25:50.480 --> 00:25:51.320
 That's so funny.

00:25:51.320 --> 00:25:51.840
 Yeah.

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:55.000
 And then life for 125 years, women were not allowed to do it

00:25:55.000 --> 00:25:56.480
 until 2021.

00:25:56.480 --> 00:25:58.360
 It's quite recent, is there?

00:25:58.360 --> 00:26:02.440
 Oh, it's so long ago now, 2021.

00:26:02.440 --> 00:26:06.640
 And the winner was Lizzie Dain, and who's British, I think.

00:26:06.640 --> 00:26:08.480
 And the sad thing about that was--

00:26:08.480 --> 00:26:12.160
 and this in fairness, this happens in a lot of cycle races,

00:26:12.160 --> 00:26:15.680
 which is you don't watch the first hour or so,

00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:16.720
 because it's quite boring.

00:26:16.720 --> 00:26:17.800
 They're just going really slowly.

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:19.960
 And then someone attacks, and then it gets interesting.

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:22.800
 But she attacked at the very first sector.

00:26:22.800 --> 00:26:24.480
 And so by the time the camera started,

00:26:24.480 --> 00:26:26.160
 she was already so far ahead that no one

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:27.680
 was ever going to catch her.

00:26:27.680 --> 00:26:31.080
 And so the entire broadcast was just her being miles

00:26:31.080 --> 00:26:33.160
 in front of everyone else, and just winning.

00:26:33.160 --> 00:26:33.840
 Well, oh, sorry.

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:36.280
 I thought you meant the people who were filming couldn't catch

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:37.280
 her.

00:26:37.280 --> 00:26:38.120
 [LAUGHTER]

00:26:38.120 --> 00:26:40.240
 Surely they've invented cars for this race.

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:43.760
 Why don't they have an invented woman yet?

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:47.440
 She's the only British cyclist to win this race.

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:48.480
 So it's--

00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:50.520
 And was she-- sorry, was she winning the mixed mix?

00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:52.400
 No, no, no, the women's--

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:54.440
 Yeah, so 2021 was the first women's race.

00:26:54.440 --> 00:26:55.760
 She won that.

00:26:55.760 --> 00:26:58.320
 Bradley Wiggins has done it quite a few times.

00:26:58.320 --> 00:26:59.720
 I think he came ninth.

00:26:59.720 --> 00:27:01.600
 He was the closest that he ever got.

00:27:01.600 --> 00:27:02.600
 Yeah.

00:27:02.600 --> 00:27:03.800
 Not very good.

00:27:03.800 --> 00:27:04.320
 Sorry?

00:27:04.320 --> 00:27:05.040
 Not very good, is it?

00:27:05.040 --> 00:27:06.040
 Not coming home.

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:06.560
 No.

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:07.560
 Coming home.

00:27:07.560 --> 00:27:08.680
 [LAUGHTER]

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:09.520
 Do you know what?

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:10.680
 He's in a team.

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:11.720
 He's in Sky team.

00:27:11.720 --> 00:27:13.440
 That's what he wrote for us.

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:14.840
 Yeah, he was in Sky team.

00:27:14.840 --> 00:27:17.840
 Do you know what the first professional team he was in

00:27:17.840 --> 00:27:19.360
 when he started racing?

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:20.640
 Because they're in teams, Andy.

00:27:20.640 --> 00:27:21.480
 You're in like a--

00:27:21.480 --> 00:27:22.280
 You get like--

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:23.240
 All right, I'm still.

00:27:23.240 --> 00:27:24.040
 OK.

00:27:24.040 --> 00:27:30.800
 It was the Linda McCartney racing team, which focused largely

00:27:30.800 --> 00:27:34.440
 on racers who were vegetarians as well as the crew.

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:35.440
 Wow.

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:37.120
 Well, I mean, talking about women's cycling,

00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:39.040
 and the reason I asked if it was the mixed race,

00:27:39.040 --> 00:27:41.800
 is that actually cycling is one of the things

00:27:41.800 --> 00:27:45.440
 that women and men could be on a par at in future.

00:27:45.440 --> 00:27:48.520
 And in fact, there's this thing called the RAM, which

00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:51.040
 is the race across America.

00:27:51.040 --> 00:27:52.360
 And--

00:27:52.360 --> 00:27:54.800
 It is R-A-A-M, isn't it?

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:55.600
 Yeah, it is.

00:27:55.600 --> 00:27:56.200
 Yeah.

00:27:56.200 --> 00:27:57.120
 Race across America.

00:27:57.120 --> 00:27:59.440
 How America?

00:27:59.440 --> 00:28:01.640
 It's meant to be the most extreme cycling race in the world.

00:28:01.640 --> 00:28:02.560
 It's absolutely insane.

00:28:02.560 --> 00:28:04.880
 People who do it have these crazy hallucinations.

00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:05.560
 They go mad.

00:28:05.560 --> 00:28:07.880
 They start dribbling and shrieking and, you know,

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:09.640
 thinking they're being murdered and stuff.

00:28:09.640 --> 00:28:14.520
 But it's 3,000 miles--

00:28:14.520 --> 00:28:15.480
 it's across America.

00:28:15.480 --> 00:28:17.800
 You're not allowed to take longer than 12 days.

00:28:17.800 --> 00:28:24.080
 And the winners of the RAM in 2021 and 2023 were both women.

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:26.360
 And that's mixed gender, RAM.

00:28:26.360 --> 00:28:29.240
 So 2021, it was actually--

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:32.560
 and I'm always doing this, having just 1040--

00:28:32.560 --> 00:28:34.720
 looking up things I could still achieve.

00:28:34.720 --> 00:28:39.080
 And the winner in 2021 was a woman called Lear Goldstein,

00:28:39.080 --> 00:28:40.600
 who was 52.

00:28:40.600 --> 00:28:42.480
 Mm-hmm.

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:43.280
 Yeah.

00:28:43.280 --> 00:28:45.560
 Well, she doesn't listen.

00:28:45.560 --> 00:28:46.840
 It's fine.

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:47.920
 Was she American?

00:28:47.920 --> 00:28:49.400
 I think so, yeah.

00:28:49.400 --> 00:28:51.200
 She-- oh, maybe she was--

00:28:51.200 --> 00:28:52.560
 I know, I think she was Israeli, because she

00:28:52.560 --> 00:28:55.520
 used to be a quantum weight kickboxing champion in Israel,

00:28:55.520 --> 00:28:57.560
 which, to be fair, I haven't achieved before the age of 40.

00:28:57.560 --> 00:28:58.360
 And she had.

00:28:58.360 --> 00:28:59.360
 Right.

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:01.840
 And she was also the 1998 duathlon champion.

00:29:01.840 --> 00:29:03.960
 But aside from that, I could do it.

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:05.040
 Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:29:05.040 --> 00:29:09.040
 There was the very first RAM had only four races in it.

00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:11.520
 And one of them was a guy called Michael Shermer.

00:29:11.520 --> 00:29:14.080
 And I don't think he's won the RAM, but he

00:29:14.080 --> 00:29:17.320
 did invent a new medical problem off the back of something

00:29:17.320 --> 00:29:18.560
 he experienced.

00:29:18.560 --> 00:29:19.800
 You know what I mean?

00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:21.920
 You know how you sort of hope that one day something

00:29:21.920 --> 00:29:24.560
 horrible is going to be named after you in a medical sense?

00:29:24.560 --> 00:29:26.040
 Is it a cycling injury?

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:28.200
 Yeah, it's called Shermer's Neck.

00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:32.240
 Oh, that's not the part of the body I thought you were going to go.

00:29:32.240 --> 00:29:35.960
 So Shermer's Neck is a thing when you've been riding so long,

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:39.280
 and your body has really, really gone to its edge.

00:29:39.280 --> 00:29:44.120
 That suddenly your neck just gives up, and your head just drops.

00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:45.080
 Oh, my God.

00:29:45.080 --> 00:29:46.520
 And you can't do anything.

00:29:46.520 --> 00:29:47.320
 And it's just gone.

00:29:47.320 --> 00:29:49.160
 The muscles are entirely gone.

00:29:49.160 --> 00:29:52.800
 And so Shermer had to hold his head up for the rest of the race.

00:29:52.800 --> 00:29:54.240
 Yeah, with one hand on the handlebars.

00:29:54.240 --> 00:29:56.400
 And he's not the only person to have this.

00:29:56.400 --> 00:29:58.640
 So multiple people have had Shermer's Neck.

00:29:58.640 --> 00:30:01.680
 And you can even buy devices.

00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:02.720
 Because they don't give up.

00:30:02.720 --> 00:30:04.000
 What's their neck keeps up?

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:04.840
 Cyclists.

00:30:04.840 --> 00:30:09.120
 They pull over and they put on a device that just pulls their head up.

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:13.640
 And they just ride the rest of this insane thing that they're doing.

00:30:13.640 --> 00:30:16.520
 At least at the end of the race, it's quite easy to put the metal over him.

00:30:16.520 --> 00:30:29.000
 OK, it is time for fact number three.

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:30.560
 And that is my fact.

00:30:30.560 --> 00:30:36.000
 My fact this week is that 150 years before there was a social network

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:46.720
 called X, run by a man called Musk, there was a social network called X, run by a man called Busk.

00:30:46.720 --> 00:30:47.720
 Insane.

00:30:47.720 --> 00:30:48.720
 Thank you.

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:57.200
 So Elon Musk named X, an Ellen Busk named the other X from 150 years ago.

00:30:57.200 --> 00:31:01.160
 And basically, I was doing research about Albomile Street, the street that are on.

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:03.440
 Which this is an amazing road, by the way.

00:31:03.440 --> 00:31:05.600
 There's so many incredible things that have happened here.

00:31:05.600 --> 00:31:10.680
 There was a publishers just next door where Byron was published by them.

00:31:10.680 --> 00:31:14.040
 And when he died, all of his memoirs and papers were burnt.

00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:16.360
 That happened just next door over there.

00:31:16.360 --> 00:31:22.160
 There was the Albomile Club where Oscar Wilde was accused of being a Sodomite.

00:31:22.160 --> 00:31:24.520
 And that happened literally on the road that we're on.

00:31:24.520 --> 00:31:26.760
 That ruined his life sent him to jail.

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:31.560
 So while I was reading that, I discovered that on the 3rd of November, 1864,

00:31:31.560 --> 00:31:37.280
 at St. George's Hotel on this road, a social network was set up called the X Club.

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:42.680
 And it was run by nine great scientists, one of whom was George Busk.

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:45.880
 This was one of the very first scientific groups.

00:31:45.880 --> 00:31:48.520
 And they called it a social network at the time.

00:31:48.520 --> 00:31:53.720
 And the idea was they could see that science was being excluded from so many places

00:31:53.720 --> 00:31:58.320
 where it was needed in terms of political power in schools and so on.

00:31:58.320 --> 00:32:02.600
 And they thought let's get together and start discussing how we can inject science into these places.

00:32:02.600 --> 00:32:07.600
 So literally around the corner, they changed the landscape of how this country operated

00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:09.800
 because they were all very influential people.

00:32:09.800 --> 00:32:13.760
 George Busk and his wife, Ellen, were very much a part of this.

00:32:13.760 --> 00:32:18.920
 Thomas Huxley was the original John Lubbock, a lot of people who were lectured in this very room.

00:32:18.920 --> 00:32:24.440
 And Ellen called it the X Club because she said, you need a name where you can just change

00:32:24.440 --> 00:32:26.000
 and adapt to what the club is.

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:27.840
 You don't want to get stuck into one thing.

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:30.600
 You can talk about anything because X can be anything.

00:32:30.600 --> 00:32:33.560
 Two quick thoughts on the name.

00:32:33.560 --> 00:32:37.040
 Firstly, should have called themselves the X-Men because they were all men.

00:32:37.040 --> 00:32:41.480
 The second point, there were nine of them and the symbol is X, which is, of course, Latin

00:32:41.480 --> 00:32:42.480
 4.

00:32:42.480 --> 00:32:43.480
 Nine.

00:32:43.480 --> 00:32:44.480
 Yeah.

00:32:44.480 --> 00:32:48.760
 I'm just seeing two big missed opportunities here.

00:32:48.760 --> 00:32:51.320
 You want to go to the one X Club, the I X Club.

00:32:51.320 --> 00:32:52.320
 The I X Men.

00:32:52.320 --> 00:32:53.320
 I don't think.

00:32:53.320 --> 00:32:59.160
 Are you invite one more person in, then there's ten of you.

00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:02.560
 Well, they kept on trying to invite people in, didn't they?

00:33:02.560 --> 00:33:03.560
 They kept on...

00:33:03.560 --> 00:33:05.560
 You need just one more.

00:33:05.560 --> 00:33:08.360
 We don't like anyone else.

00:33:08.360 --> 00:33:12.160
 There was this constant chat, as you can imagine, they were saying, look, should we get

00:33:12.160 --> 00:33:15.680
 barry in or, you know, should we get, you know, Dorothy?

00:33:15.680 --> 00:33:19.480
 And eventually, they, apparently, various attempts to add more members became so

00:33:19.480 --> 00:33:24.000
 weirysome, this is according to Huxley, that Huxley made this new rule.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:28.480
 He said that no idea of someone joining the club should be entertained unless the name

00:33:28.480 --> 00:33:33.640
 of the new member contains all the consonants absent from the old ones.

00:33:33.640 --> 00:33:34.640
